Create a mobile-first photo archive using Three.js, where the entire phone viewport behaves like an old CRT television screen.

Do NOT render or model a physical television.

The user's phone itself should feel like the CRT display.

The experience should resemble watching old personal photographs and home videos through an early-2000s television or camcorder monitor.

Use Three.js primarily for a full-screen custom ShaderMaterial, not for a 3D scene.

Implement the display using:

OrthographicCamera
full-screen PlaneGeometry
custom ShaderMaterial
dynamically loaded photo textures

The CRT shader should include only subtle effects:

slight barrel distortion
curved / softened screen edges
faint scanlines
analog grain
subtle luminance flicker
very slight RGB separation
vignette
occasional horizontal instability
slight chromatic softness

The photograph must remain the dominant visual element.

The desired priority is:

beautiful photograph → analog screen texture → CRT effect

Never make the effect look like glitch art.

Layout

The photograph should occupy almost the entire mobile viewport.

Avoid large frames, decorative device mockups, cards, borders, or fake television bodies.

The interface should feel extremely minimal.

Overlay small camcorder / television OSD typography directly over the photograph:

PLAY ▶
CH 03

2009.08.21
SP

Use small off-white text with a very subtle shadow.

Do not use modern UI components, glassmorphism, pills, cards, carousel dots, large buttons, or navigation bars.

Navigation

Swipe left or right to move between photographs.

Do not use standard sliding carousel animation.

Every photograph should behave like a television channel.

Transition sequence:

current image
→ slight horizontal compression/distortion
→ 100–180 ms analog static
→ next image appears
→ CRT stabilizes

Keep the entire transition short and tactile.

Tapping the right side of the screen advances to the next photograph.
Tapping the left side goes back.

Initial experience

On first load, display a black CRT screen.

After the user's first tap:

black
→ bright horizontal line
→ line expands vertically
→ brief analog static
→ first photograph

Recreate the characteristic CRT power-on animation.

Do not show an artificial power button.

Photograph presentation

Preserve the original aspect ratio of each photograph whenever possible.

Portrait photographs should work naturally on portrait mobile screens.

Landscape photographs may be letterboxed rather than aggressively cropped.

The empty area should be dark and visually blend into the CRT display.

Older low-resolution images should not be artificially sharpened. Their imperfections are part of the aesthetic.

Interaction details

On press-and-hold, temporarily reduce scanlines, noise, distortion and OSD opacity so the user can inspect the original photograph more clearly.

When released, smoothly restore the CRT treatment.

Optional subtle feedback:

a tiny screen brightness pulse when changing channels
slight static audio after user interaction
subtle haptic feedback on supported mobile devices

Audio must remain muted until the user explicitly interacts with the page.

Art direction

Avoid:

cyber Y2K
vaporwave
neon
VHS glitch overload
retro game UI
pixel-art aesthetics
fake Windows 98 UI
physical TV mockups

Instead reference:

Korean home videos from the late 1990s and early 2000s
family camcorder footage
old digital camera photographs
analog television broadcasts
personal memory archives

The visual tone should be intimate, quiet, imperfect and nostalgic.

It should feel as though old memories are being broadcast directly through the user's phone.

Performance

Optimize aggressively for mobile Safari and Chrome.

Cap device pixel ratio around 1.5–2.

Preload only the current, previous and next photograph.

Dispose unused textures.

Use compressed WebP or AVIF images.

Pause shader animation when the page is hidden.

Respect prefers-reduced-motion.

Avoid expensive post-processing unless absolutely necessary.

The end result should not look like a website themed around an old television.

It should feel like the phone itself has temporarily become an old screen containing someone's memories.




Page structure and gesture behavior

The Three.js CRT photo gallery is only the first section of the page, not the entire website.

Structure the page like:

┌──────────────────────────┐
│                          │
│    Three.js CRT Gallery  │  ← approximately 100svh
│                          │
│   ← swipe photos →       │
│                          │
└──────────────────────────┘
             ↓
       normal page scroll
             ↓
┌──────────────────────────┐
│ Existing DOM content     │
│ wedding information      │
│ text / sections / etc.   │
└──────────────────────────┘

The Three.js canvas must participate in the normal document flow.

Do not create a full-page fixed canvas that blocks scrolling.

The gallery section should be around 100svh, followed immediately by the existing HTML/DOM sections.

Gesture rules

Horizontal swipe:

swipe left  → next photo
swipe right → previous photo

Vertical swipe:

swipe up/down → normal browser page scroll

This distinction is extremely important.

The gallery must never trap the user inside the Three.js area.

Do not call preventDefault() globally on touch events.

Use CSS similar to:

canvas {
  touch-action: pan-y;
}

This allows the browser to retain native vertical scrolling while the app interprets horizontal gestures.

Gesture detection

Determine gesture direction only after the pointer has moved past a small threshold.

Example logic:

dx = currentX - startX
dy = currentY - startY

if abs(dx) > threshold
   and abs(dx) > abs(dy) * 1.2:
       horizontal gesture

otherwise:
       allow normal vertical scrolling

Use a swipe threshold of approximately 30–50px.

Do not change photographs for small accidental horizontal movement while the user is scrolling vertically.

Once a gesture has clearly been classified as vertical, Three.js should stop processing that pointer gesture entirely.

Important mobile UX

The user should be able to place their finger anywhere over the photograph and naturally scroll downward into the next page section.

They should not need to search for a special scroll area outside the canvas.

On a diagonal gesture, prioritize vertical page scrolling unless horizontal intention is obvious.

In other words:

vertical scrolling has priority over photo navigation.

Horizontal photo navigation should only activate when the gesture is clearly horizontal.

Do not implement vertical swipe gestures inside the gallery.

Transition behavior

When a horizontal swipe is accepted:

user drags horizontally
→ current photograph reacts very slightly
→ finger releases
→ brief CRT horizontal distortion/static
→ next or previous photograph

Do not physically slide the whole photograph across the screen like a conventional carousel.

The user's swipe is an input gesture, but the visual transition should still resemble changing a television channel.

A very small horizontal displacement while dragging is acceptable, but once the threshold is reached the actual transition should be CRT distortion/static rather than a carousel slide.

Scroll transition into the next section

As the gallery approaches the bottom of the viewport, do not add a special Three.js transition.

Let the Three.js scene naturally move upward with the document as the user scrolls.

The next DOM section should simply enter from below through normal native scrolling.

Avoid:

scroll hijacking
scroll snapping
pinned canvas sections
forced full-screen transitions
GSAP-style scroll-controlled scene changes

This should feel like a normal mobile webpage whose first section happens to contain an unusual interactive CRT photo viewer.